GIMP Palette
Name: MonoGame.GameFramework + red (car pipeline test)
Columns: 6
#
# THIS IS NOT THE CANONICAL PALETTE. assets/palette.gpl is.
#
# This file is scoped to the car pipeline test in this directory and nothing
# else. It is the canonical 13 colours, byte-identical and in their original
# order, plus a 5-step red ramp that the canonical palette does not have.
#
# Why it exists
# -------------
# The canonical palette carries four ramps -- outline, warm, blue, and the
# single accent-rust -- and no red. Conforming a red car against it measured:
#
#     mean delta       0.0840   (threshold 0.05 -> "regenerate, don't fix")
#     recoloured       100%
#     palette coverage 8/13
#     accent-rust      214 px, 31% of the sprite
#
# The last line is the real problem. STYLE.md budgets accent-rust at "one
# element, one place, under 2%"; a red car spends 31% of itself on it, and the
# rest of the body scatters across warm-0-shadow, warm-2-mid and warm-3 -- three
# different ramps, which breaks "shade within one ramp" outright. The output is
# a brown car with a tan roof and blue wheels. The gate was right to reject it.
#
# Why the reds are these reds
# ---------------------------
# Hue is held at 351-5 degrees, low enough that every red stays more than 15
# degrees from the warm ramp (26-34) and from accent-rust (12), so nothing lands
# in an existing hue family. Values are spaced 27/43/66/85/96 to dodge
# accent-rust's 47.8 by a wide margin. Checked with the same algorithm
# Palette.FindRampCollisions uses: 0 collisions across all 18 colours, and the
# tightest red pair sits 11.0 apart in value where the shipped blue-4/blue-5
# pair sits 3.1 apart.
#
# Adopting this into assets/palette.gpl is a real decision, not a formality --
# that palette is deliberately the *observed* set of colours in shipped art, and
# no shipped sprite is red. Merge these five rows only when something red
# actually ships.
#
